{'data': {'actor_id': '01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771', 'created_at': '2026-09-22T10:00:30.545Z', 'event_type': 'comment.status_changed', 'id': '96', 'payload': {'from': 'open', 'reason': "CONFIRMED, and the fix goes further than the finding asked — 158ce1a3. This was a regression I introduced by merging global facts into the ranked set without noticing what sat downstream of it.\n\nThe mechanism is exactly as described: the caches are keyed by (brainDir, branch), `flush` writes only the ids touched this run and prunes the rest, so a global fact filed under one repository's key is both stored where it does not belong and pruned out again the moment that repo queries with global facts off.\n\n**The cited precedent is half a guard, and copying it would have copied the hole.** recall filtered the slice passed to `retain` — but `factVector` (embed_rank.go:109) does `s.touched[f.ID] = true` *and* `s.cache[f.ID] = v` while ranking, long before `retain` is reached. So recall was already persisting global vectors on any run that embedded one; the filter only suppressed the second, redundant marking. Fixing my two call sites the same way would have looked correct and left the defect reachable through ranking on all three.\n\nSo the rule moved into the reranker, which is the only place that can enforce it:\n\n- `markForeign(ids)` records ids that rank here but belong to another store\n- `factVector` embeds a foreign fact and returns it **without** caching or touching\n- `retain` and `markT
